# SDOC Feature Suggestions @sdoc-suggestions
{
    # Meta @meta
    {
        type: doc

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    # About @about
    {
        Proposed additions to the SDOC spec. Each suggestion references
        requirements it serves, includes priority, and explains rationale.
        Check here before designing new features.
    }

    # Context @context
    {
        This document suggests additions to the SDOC spec based on gaps identified in v0.1. Each suggestion references the requirements it serves (see [requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc)). Suggestions are ordered by priority.

        When a suggestion is accepted and implemented, it is retained here for historical context. The specification.sdoc carries the normative definition.
    }

    # S2: Document Metadata Beyond Styling @s2
    {
        Priority: High (partially addressed)
        Serves: R5, R6, R7 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        The \@meta scope currently handles style, header, and footer. Richer metadata (author, date, version, status, tags) is now supported via key:value syntax inside the \@meta scope. Both sub-scope and key:value styles work:

        ```
        # Meta @meta
        {
            author: Jane Smith
            date: 2026-02-06
            version: 0.3
            status: Draft
            tags: specification, api, v2
            style: styles/custom.css
        }
        ```

        Remaining work: defining a canonical set of well-known keys and ensuring tooling consistently exposes `meta.properties` to downstream consumers (renderers, AI agents, search indices).
    }

    # S3: Comments @s3
    {
        Priority: High
        Serves: R5, R8 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        {+Implemented in v0.2.+} Both approaches were adopted:

        {[.]
            - **Line comments:** `//` at line start (after optional indentation) skips the line entirely. Not in AST, not rendered. Mid-line `//` has no effect (URLs safe). No effect inside code blocks.
            - **Comment scopes:** `# Title :comment` uses the scope type system. In AST but not rendered. Agents can extract them. Can contain any valid SDOC content.
        }

        This resolves the open question from the v0.1 spec. Line comments handle quick annotations; comment scopes handle structured non-rendered content.
    }

    # S4: Semantic Scope Types @s4
    {
        Priority: High
        Serves: R5, R7 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        {+Implemented in v0.2.+} Scope types use `:typename` after the optional `@id` on headings:

        ```
        # User Authentication @auth :requirement
        {
            The system shall authenticate users via OAuth 2.0.
        }

        # OAuth Implementation @auth-spec :specification
        {
            Implements @auth using the authorization code flow.
        }

        # Auth Test Plan @auth-test :test
        {
            - [ ] Test login flow
            - [ ] Test token refresh
            - [ ] Test logout
        }
        ```

        {[.]
            - Syntax: `# Title @id :type` or `# Title :type @id` or `# Title :type` — both orderings supported
            - `:type` requires whitespace before the colon (so `# Note: Important` is NOT a type)
            - Any string is valid. Well-known types: `schema`, `example`, `requirement`, `specification`, `definition`, `note`, `warning`, `test`, `task`, `api`, `config`, `deprecated`, `comment`
            - The `:comment` type creates scopes that are in the AST but not rendered (see @s3)
        }
    }

    # S5: Cross-File References @s5
    {
        Priority: Medium
        Serves: R9 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        The \@id reference system is per-file. For multi-file projects, you need to reference scopes in other files.

        Suggested approach:

        ```
        See @guide/intro#overview for the introduction.
        ```

        Where the path before # is the file (relative to project root), and after # is the scope ID within that file. The short form \@id should continue to resolve within the current file first, then optionally search the project.
    }

    # S6: Include/Transclusion @s6
    {
        Priority: Medium
        Serves: R9, R7 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        The ability to include one SDOC file (or a specific scope from it) inside another. Essential for large documents assembled from modular parts.

        Possible syntax:

        ```
        # Full Project Documentation
        {
            @include(architecture/overview.sdoc)
            @include(architecture/design.sdoc#api-layer)
        }
        ```

        This keeps the source files independent and editable, while allowing composed views. The rendered output inlines the included content at the specified position.
    }

    # S7: Definition Lists @s7
    {
        Priority: Medium
        Serves: R7 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        Technical specs, glossaries, and legal documents frequently use definition lists (term + definition pairs). Currently these would be modelled as scopes where the heading is the term and the body is the definition, which works but loses the semantic distinction.

        Possible syntax:

        ```
        {[def]
            - API
            { Application Programming Interface. A set of protocols for building software. }
            - SDK
            { Software Development Kit. A collection of tools for a specific platform. }
        }
        ```

        This follows the existing list type pattern ({[.]} for bullet, {[#]} for numbered, {[def]} for definition).
    }

    # S8: Admonitions/Callouts @s8
    {
        Priority: Low
        Serves: R7 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        Warning, note, tip, and caution boxes are common in technical documentation. This is handled by scope type annotations (see @s4), making a dedicated syntax unnecessary:

        ```
        # Important @note1 :warning
        {
            This API is deprecated and will be removed in v2.0.
        }
        ```

        The rendering pipeline would style :warning, :note, :tip, :caution scopes with appropriate visual treatment.
    }

    # S10: Extended Meta Properties for Agent Navigation @s10
    {
        Priority: Medium
        Serves: R5, R9 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        The \`\@about\` scope provides a prose discovery summary that works well for both humans and agents (~50-80 tokens). The \`\@meta\` scope carries structured metadata like \`type\`, \`tags\`, and \`status\`. Currently the set of well-known meta keys is small and focused on presentation (style, header, footer) or basic classification (type, tags, status).

        Extending \`\@meta\` with richer structured fields would help AI agents make faster navigation decisions without reading \`\@about\` or section headings. The key insight: \`\@about\` should remain prose (it serves both audiences well and is already cheap to consume), but \`\@meta\` should carry the structured data that enables programmatic filtering and relationship discovery.

        Suggested additional well-known keys:

        ```
        # Meta @meta
        {
            type: doc
            tags: authentication, api, security
            status: active

            audience: contributors, security-reviewers
            depends-on: @user-model, @api-gateway
            related: architecture.sdoc, spec.sdoc
            read-when: adding-auth-features, reviewing-security
        }
        ```

        {[.]
            - `audience` — who this document is for, enabling agents to skip irrelevant files based on their current task
            - `depends-on` — references to other documents or sections this document assumes knowledge of, enabling agents to load prerequisites first
            - `related` — sibling documents that cover adjacent topics, enabling agents to discover relevant context
            - `read-when` — task descriptions that indicate when this document is useful, enabling agents to match documents to their current intent without parsing prose
        }

        These fields complement rather than replace \`\@about\`. The prose summary carries nuance and intent for both humans and agents. The structured fields enable fast programmatic filtering — an agent with a task list could match \`read-when\` values against its current task without language comprehension, then read \`\@about\` only for the matches.

        Design constraints:
        {[.]
            - All values should be plain text or comma-separated lists — no nested structure in meta values
            - Keys should be lowercase kebab-case
            - Tooling (MCP servers, index generators) should expose these fields in discovery responses
            - Unrecognised keys are preserved as custom properties — the set is extensible by convention
        }
    }

    # S9: Reserved Syntax Space @s9
    {
        Priority: Low
        Serves: R10 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        The spec should explicitly document which character sequences are reserved for future use, even if they don't currently have meaning. This prevents existing documents from accidentally using syntax that later becomes meaningful.

        Candidates for reservation:
        {[.]
            - {[...]} patterns (for future list/scope types)
            - \@keyword patterns at line start (for directives like \@include)
            - `:word` is now used for scope types (v0.2, see @s4). `::` remains reserved for potential future use.
        }
    }

    # S11: Optical Left-Margin Alignment @s11
    {
        Priority: Low
        Serves: R3 ([requirements.sdoc](requirements.sdoc))

        A slide's kicker, title and lede all start at the same box edge and
        their ink does not, because each first glyph carries a different left
        side bearing. Boxes align; ink does not; the reader sees the ink.

        Proposed and measured by a deck author rather than inferred here. The
        measurements are the valuable part, so they are recorded rather than
        summarised.

        # Why a per-font constant does not work @s11-why
        {
            First-letter bearings measured from a live render with canvas
            \`actualBoundingBoxLeft\`, in em:

            {[table]
                Shape | Letters | Bearing
                flat-sided | B D E I M P R | 0.090
                round | C S | 0.039–0.047
                diagonal or open | A T W | 0.018–0.024
            }

            The spread is 0.074em, which at a 68px title is 5.03px — larger
            than the correction itself. In the deck that prompted this, eight
            of 26 titles began with A, T or W, so one constant would be wrong
            by up to 5px on nearly a third of the deck.

            Pure ink alignment is also wrong: a round C or a diagonal A is
            meant to overhang slightly, and setting its ink flush against a
            flat-sided P reads as indented. A hand-rolled version in a company
            theme had already discovered this and added a manual nudge back to
            the right, with different values for display and small text.
        }

        # The rule @s11-rule
        {
            Shift each line right by \`max_lsb - lsb_i\` — the difference
            between its own bearing and the largest bearing in the group —
            with a small optical allowance adjusting the group as a whole.

            Expressed as a relative correction rather than an absolute one,
            flat-sided letters take most of the shift, round letters a little
            and A/T/W almost none, out of one formula rather than a per-glyph
            table that goes stale whenever a title's first letter changes.

            It applies within a group of lines meant to share a margin — the
            head — not to all left-aligned text, which would have each block
            optically aligning against itself rather than against anything a
            reader perceives as a column.
        }

        # What the formats do with it @s11-formats
        {
            Measured, not assumed, and it decides the implementation.

            {[table]
                Approach | Box moves | Survives the PPTX export
                \`text-indent\` | No | No
                \`margin\` | Yes | Yes
            }

            The PowerPoint export is measured rather than drawn: it records
            each box and places the original text or picture there.
            \`text-indent\` shifts glyphs inside a box that does not move, so
            the exporter never sees it. A margin moves the box, so it carries.
            \`text-indent\` also indents only the first line, which leaves a
            wrapping title aligned on line one and not on line two.

            Neither survives the Claude Slides export: that subset has neither
            property.

            The direction matters too. Pulling the widest-bearing line *left*
            moves its box outside the content margin and the layout check
            reports it — correctly, since optical alignment means boxes
            deliberately do not align. Shifting the other lines *right*
            instead keeps every box inside the margin, so the correction is
            both export-safe and check-safe and needs no negative values.
        }

        # Prior art to retire @s11-prior
        {
            A company deck theme hand-codes this for one title slide with a
            measured per-glyph table and a warning that changing the first
            letter invalidates it, and a deck stylesheet carries a single
            measured pixel value with the same warning. Both are what this
            would delete.

            Both are also test cases — but "it matches today" is the wrong
            success criterion for them, because both are written in
            \`text-indent\` and are therefore already absent from those decks'
            PowerPoint exports. Nobody had looked.
        }
    }
}
